+// 2026-09-03: with the governor clock-gate Removal-timing fix in place
+// (stop_clk_reg pblock), ARVALID/RVALID now succeed on ~78% of boot
+// attempts rather than 0% - but not reliably 100%. A research pass found
+// a SECOND, structurally identical hazard: ca53_cpu_reset.v (the "CPU
+// reset repeater", instantiated once per CPU as u_ca53_cpu_rst) re-syncs
+// the governor's already-pipelined reset through a single, unpipelined
+// ca53_cell_sync - same async-reset-driven-by-the-same-net-it-resyncs
+// pattern as the original bug, on the same CPU-gated-clock domain - with
+// zero further margin before fanning out to reset_n/po_reset_n, which
+// gate clock-enables throughout the DPU/BIU/DCU/IFU/TLB/STB. Unlike the
+// original bug, we have NOT added any placement fix for this one yet -
+// these two taps are purely diagnostic, to see whether it's a real
+// contributor to the remaining ~22% of failed boot attempts.
+//   - reset_chain_released_dbg: the resolved synchronizer output inside
+//     the reset repeater itself (u_ca53_cell_sync1.r1) - the exact
+//     hazard-carrying flop, analogous to stop_clk_reg in the original
+//     bug.
+//   - ifu_lfb_clk_en_dbg: the linefill buffer's clock-gate-enable
+//     register (ca53ifu_lfb.v) - gated by the same reset_n net. Chosen
+//     specifically because the I-cache is cold at boot, so the very
+//     first fetch is guaranteed to miss and must go through the LFB to
+//     receive data back from the BIU; if this clock gate fails to
+//     release, the first fetch's data could never be captured, directly
+//     explaining a "never reaches first fetch" symptom even if
+//     stop_clk_reg's own fix works correctly that boot.
+wire reset_chain_released_dbg = u_megasoc_cpu_ss.u_cortexa53.g_ca53_cpu[0].u_ca53_cpu.u_ca53_noram.u_ca53_cpu_rst.u_ca53_cell_sync1.r1;
+wire ifu_lfb_clk_en_dbg = u_megasoc_cpu_ss.u_cortexa53.g_ca53_cpu[0].u_ca53_cpu.u_ca53_noram.u_ca53ifu.u_ca53ifu_lfb.clk_lfb_en;
+
+// 2026-09-03: with reset_chain_released_dbg and ifu_lfb_clk_en_dbg BOTH
+// confirmed succeeding on 4-5 real hardware boot attempts that still
+// never asserted ARVALID (see git history / hardware log), the
+// ca53_cpu_reset.v hazard is ruled out as the explanation for the
+// remaining ~22% failure rate. A research pass traced a DIFFERENT,
+// stronger lead instead: ca53biu_addr_req_arbiter.v's scu_ar_block_i can
+// unconditionally block every AR request - including the very first
+// fetch - and its root driver is a mandatory post-reset SCU tag-RAM
+// invalidation handshake that EVERY CPU goes through
+// (ca53governor_cpu_slice.v: inv_scu_tag_state FSM). On reset, this FSM
